<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Galatia</h1><p class="st-meta">278 BCE – 25 BCE (approx.) · capital Ancyra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Galatia, the land of three Celtic tribes who crossed into Anatolia in 278 BCE and kept their own language and laws for centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 278 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-i-of-bithynia">Nicomedes I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bithynia">Kingdom of Bithynia</a> invited bands of Celtic warriors across the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporus</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. After years of raiding western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, they were defeated by Antiochus I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-pergamon">Kingdom of Pergamon</a> and settled on the central plateau around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ancyra</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Three tribes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tolistobogii">Tolistobogii</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/volcae-tectosages">Tectosages</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trocmi">Trocmi</a>, each split into four parts under tetrarchs, met in a council of 300 at a sacred grove called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drynemeton">Drynemeton</a>. Galatians served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> across the Hellenistic world. The statues of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dying-gaul">Dying Gaul</a> commemorate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon's</a> victories over them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1st century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deiotarus">Deiotarus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> ally, made himself king of all Galatia. After the death of the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amyntas-of-galatia">Amyntas</a>, in 25 BCE, Augustus made Galatia a Roman province; Paul later wrote to its Christian communities.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/galatia.jpg" alt="Galatia" width="320" height="209"><figcaption>Image: SilverStar54 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Galatia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>278 BCE – 25 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ancyra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Deiotarus, Amyntas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Galatian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Galatians, Kingdom of Galatia, Galatian tetrarchies</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-152&amp;focus=galatia" aria-label="Galatia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5489 748 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Galatia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5809 1004L5673 1031M5809 1004L5304 942M5809 1004L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5673 1031h0M5304 942h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5809 1004h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5809 1004h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ancyra. <a href="/atlas?year=-152&amp;focus=galatia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
